      T = 2 canonical: p_local (measured from the required slip gradient) =   2.96   vs   p_connect (needed to reach the galaxy bound) =   6.99   ratio 2.36
      T = 2 alt      : p_local (measured from the required slip gradient) =   3.17   vs   p_connect (needed to reach the galaxy bound) =   6.54   ratio 2.06
      T = 4 canonical: p_local (measured from the required slip gradient) =   4.25   vs   p_connect (needed to reach the galaxy bound) =   5.39   ratio 1.27
      T = 4 alt      : p_local (measured from the required slip gradient) =   4.60   vs   p_connect (needed to reach the galaxy bound) =   5.42   ratio 1.18
  [FAIL] C4 [test] the weight can be ONE power law: the slope measured at cluster depths matches the slope needed to connect down to galaxies (within 1.5x)   (p_connect / p_local = 1.18 - 14.57 over T = 1-4 and both footings.  THIS ONE IS CONVENTION-DEPENDENT and is reported as such, not banked: at the loosest truncation the two determinations agree to 1.18x, at the tightest they differ by 15x.  What is convention-free is that p_local is MEASURED at 1.5 - 4.6, i.e. the weight is genuinely steeper than the chameleon/symmetron 1-3 at cluster depths on most conventions.  Whether a single power law also reaches the galaxy bound is left open here)

      Neither of these uses the five-cluster scatter of the required weight, which L51 C13b showed is
      noise-limited (fractional error 1.41) and which this lane does not quote in either direction.


======================================================================================================================
PART D -- THE SYSTEMS IN BETWEEN: groups, disc outskirts, dwarfs, wide binaries
======================================================================================================================

  A weight that is off in galaxies and on in clusters must pass smoothly through the systems in between,
  and those are measured.  The weight is pinned at both ends by C1 -- W(X_gal_max) = the galaxy bound,
  W(X_cl) = the measured required cluster slip -- so everything below is a PREDICTION with no freedom
  left, evaluated on data, on the covariant trigger, on both footings, over the full T range.

  TWO QUANTITIES, kept separate throughout.  The EXCURSION E = W/(2X) is what an isolated object suffers
  from its own well; the pinning fixes it at the galaxy end, so it is not an independent test there.
  The RESPONSE R = p E is what a small object sitting INSIDE an ambient well suffers.  Cassini and the
  wide binaries measure R; galaxy-galaxy lensing of isolated galaxies measures E.

      D1 GROUPS: 20 X-ray groups (Lovisari, Reiprich & Schellenberger 2015, Tables 1-2), M_b = M_gas500 x 1.30 (a stated stellar correction), M_b held constant beyond R500 -- conservative, since the real gas mass keeps rising and would DEEPEN them
